      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment. It's a GLM-OCR output of pages 2-3 of OIML Certificate R76/2006-DK3-17.09 from DELTA (DK3, Denmark).\n\nKey info:\n- Certificate number: R76/2006-DK3-17.09\n- Issuing authority: DELTA, OIML Issuing Authority DK3 (Denmark)\n- Address: Venlighedsvej 4, 2970 Hørsholm, Denmark\n- Phone: +45 7219 4000\n- Email: weighing@delta.dk\n- Website: www.madebydelta.com\n- Date of issue: 27 November 2017\n- Person signed: J. Hovgård Jensen, Certification Manager\n- Test report: DELTA, DK, No. DANAK-1916850, dated 12-09-2016 (60 pages)\n- OIML issuer ID: DK3\n\nNote the markdown contains GLM chatbot commentary — I should ignore that (\"Thank you — I've noted...\", \"Is there anything specific...\", \"Summary:\"). I should extract the certificate data.\n\nCertificate characteristics (page 2):\n- Accuracy class: III\n- Weighing range: Single-interval or dual-range\n- Maximum capacity Maxᵢ: 1.5 kg to 30 kg → range {min: 1.5, max: 30}, unit kg\n- Minimum capacity Minᵢ: 20 e → value \"20e\" — hmm, 20 e means 20 verification scale intervals. Could record as value \"20 e\" with unit \"e\". Per rules, formula/symbols keep as string. I'll record value: 20, unit_symbol: \"e\"? Actually \"20 e\" means 20 times e — the unit is \"e\" (verification scale interval). I can record value: 20, unit_symbol: \"e\".\n- Verification scale interval eᵢ: ≥ 0.5 g → {min: 0.5, max: null}, unit g\n- Number of intervals Maxᵢ/eᵢ: ≤ 6000 (single-interval); ≤ 3000 per range (dual-range) — this varies by configuration → config_level! Axis: \"interval configuration\" with conditions single-interval → 6000, dual-range per range → 3000. Value: {min: null, max: 6000} etc. Actually \"≤ 6000\" is a max. Could record as value 6000 with the attribute being max_number_of_intervals... The rule for ≤: value {min: null, max: 6000}. Hmm, but for number of intervals, \"≤6000\" means maximum is 6000. I'll use {min: null, max: 6000}.\n\nActually let me think about naming: `max_number_of_intervals_maxi_over_ei` or `number_of_intervals`. Canonical label: I'll use `max_number_of_intervals`.\n\n- Subtractive tare T: ≤ −Max → value string \"-Max\"? It's \"≤ −Max\". Value as string \"≤ -Max\"? The rule says formulas/symbols keep as string. I'll record value: \"-Max\" with maybe... Actually value could be \"≤ -Max\". Keep string. Attribute: `subtractive_tare`, value \"-Max\" (max of tare subtracted). Hmm — maybe value: {min: null, max: \"-Max\"}? Simpler: value \"-Max\" as string. Actually to be careful, \"≤ −Max\" means the tare can subtract up to Max. I'll record value: \"≤ −Max\"... but no special characters issues — minus sign −. Use \"-Max\". I'll record value: {min: null, max: \"-Max\"}? That mixes. Simpler: value string \"-Max\". Let me just do {\"value\": \"-Max\"} — meaning tare up to Max subtractive.\n\nHmm, maybe better: attribute `subtractive_tare`, value: {min: null, max: \"Max\"}? That's odd too. I'll go with value string \"-Max\".\n\n- Interface: Protective, according to paragraph 5.3.6 → value string \"Protective (per 5.3.6)\" — no punctuation? Rule says no trailing punctuation. \"Protective, according to paragraph 5.3.6\" → value \"Protective according to paragraph 5.3.6\". Attribute: `interface`.\n\n- Supply voltage: 9 VDC from external adapter (100–240 VAC, 50–60 Hz); optional 6 V internal battery.\n  Split:\n  - supply_voltage: 9 V DC (main supply to indicator)\n  - adapter_input_voltage: 100–240 V AC\n  - adapter_input_frequency: 50–60 Hz\n  - battery_voltage: 6 V (optional internal battery)\n  \n- Temperature range: −10 °C / +40 °C → {min: -10, max: 40}, unit °C. Attribute: `temperature_range`.\n\n- Software identification: see page 3.\n\nSoftware table (page 3) — model family! The cert covers models: VGM, VGW, NGP, VGC, VDM, VDW, VDP, VDC, VOM, VOWK, VOP, VOC. Each with an application (WEIGHING, CHECKWEIGHING, PRICE COMPUTING, PARTS COUNTING) and software identification (CE020, CE004, etc.).\n\nSo model_family.models should include these 12 models with attributes: application, software_identification.\n\nThe table can also be a matrix_table: name \"Approved software identification\", columns: model_name, application, software_identification.\n\nCommon software version: 1.02xx where xx from 00 to 99. → type_level characteristic `common_software_version`: value \"1.02xx\" with footnote? Or as value \"1.02xx\". Could also record `common_software_version_range`. I'll do value \"1.02xx\" and a note. Actually maybe record value \"1.02xx\" with attribute common_software_version, and a note about xx 00-99. There's no footnotes array item defined for that... I could add footnote marker but there's no footnote definition. Better: value \"1.02xx (xx = 00–99)\". Hmm, keep it clean: value \"1.02xx\". And maybe a separate description. The schema's characteristics values are objects with value/unit/footnote_markers. I could add a matrix_table? No. I'll add value \"1.02xx\" and include a second characteristic `common_software_version_variant_range` value {min:0, max:99}? That seems noisy. Maybe value: \"1.02xx, xx = 00 to 99 (covers differences in non-legal applications)\". Strings are allowed. I'll keep: {\"value\": \"1.02xx (xx = 00 to 99)\"}. No trailing punctuation — fine.\n\nAlso model-level characteristics: software_identification per model is captured in models[].attributes and/or matrix_tables. I could also emit model_level characteristics for software_identification, but attributes in model_family is enough. Actually the schema example shows model_level used for varying attributes. To be safe, I'll put application and software_identification in models[].attributes and also matrix_tables. That should suffice.\n\nCertified type: category — it's a non-automatic weighing instrument (indicator). The summary says \"non-automatic weighing instrument (indicator)\". Category: \"Weighing instrument\" or \"Indicator\"? The text says \"This certificate applies to a non-automatic weighing instrument (indicator)\". Hmm — the table shows Maxᵢ, Minᵢ, eᵢ with subscript i suggesting multiple intervals/indicators. R76 covers weighing instruments. I'll use category \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\". Type designations: the model names VGM, VGW, NGP, VGC, VDM, VDW, VDP, VDC, VOM, VOWK, VOP, VOC.\n\nScheme: The certificate number R76/2006-DK3-17.09 — scheme for R76 is typically A? Actually R76 certificates — OIML cs scheme... The R76-2006 is under the OIML Certificate System (not the CS \"B\" scheme; scheme A vs B). Hmm, the old OIML certificates don't explicitly say scheme. But DK3... Under OIML-CS there are Scheme A and B. R76/2006 is part of OIML CS; certificates for R76 can be issued under both. Without explicit info, omit scheme.\n\nrecommendation: id \"R76\", edition 2006. accuracy_classes: [\"III\"].\n\ntest_reports: id \"DANAK-1916850\", date 2016-09-12 (\"12-09-2016\" — Danish format DD-MM-YYYY → 12 September 2016), pages 60, role: type_evaluation_report. Issued by DELTA, DK.\n\nPage total: 3 (\"This certificate includes 3 pages\"). The fragment says page 2 of 3 and page 3 of 3. page_total: 3.\n\nIssuing authority:\n- name: DELTA (OIML Issuing Authority DK3)\n- address_lines: [\"Venlighedsvej 4\", \"2970 Hørsholm\", \"Denmark\"]\n- person_responsible: J. Hovgård Jensen\n- person_title: Certification Manager\n- phone: +45 7219 4000\n- email: weighing@delta.dk\n- website: www.madebydelta.com\n- oiml_issuer_id: DK3\n\ncertificate:\n- number: \"R76/2006-DK3-17.09\"\n- scheme: omit (not stated)\n- page_total: 3\n- member_state: Denmark\n- date_issued: 2017-11-27\n- oiml_issuer_id: DK3\n\nApplicants/manufacturers: not stated in this fragment. Omit.\n\nComponents: not present.\n\nConfig-level: number of intervals differs between single-interval and dual-range. axis: \" weighing range configuration\" — conditions: \"single-interval\" → 6000, \"dual-range (per range)\" → 3000.\n\nStructure:\n```json\n\"config_level\": [\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"max_number_of_intervals\",\n    \"axis\": \"weighing range configuration\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"single-interval\", \"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 6000}, \"unit_symbol\": null},\n      {\"condition\": \"dual-range (per range)\", \"value\": {\"min\": null, max: 3000}, \"unit_symbol\": null}\n    ]\n  }\n]\n```\n\nHmm — is ≤6000 a range? It's \"Maximum capacity / e ≤ 6000\", i.e., the max number is 6000. Recording value 6000 with attribute max_number_of_intervals is cleaner: value: 6000. Yes! Attribute name already says \"max\", so value 6000 plain. I'll do value: 6000.\n\nSimilarly \"Verification scale interval eᵢ ≥ 0.5 g\" — attribute `verification_scale_interval_ei`, value {min: 0.5, max: null}, unit g. Good.\n\nSubtractive tare: attribute `subtractive_tare`, value \"-Max\"? Let me record as value string \"≤ -Max\"... The minus sign − (U+2212) — convert to ASCII hyphen. value: \"-Max\". Unit null. Fine.\n\nSupply voltage 9 VDC: attribute `supply_voltage`, value 9, unit \"V DC\"? Unit symbol: \"V\". Maybe add `power_supply_type: \"DC\"`? Rules: `110-240V AC 50/60Hz` split → power_supply_voltage, power_supply_frequency, power_supply_type. Here: supply_voltage 9 VDC external adapter; adapter input 100–240 VAC 50–60 Hz; battery 6 V.\n\nCharacteristics:\n- supply_voltage: 9, \"V\", maybe footnote-less. Add power_supply_type: \"DC (external adapter)\" — I'll do supply_voltage: {value: 9, unit_symbol: \"V\"}, and note \"from external adapter\" — hmm, where? Could add attribute power_supply_description. Keep simple: \n  - `supply_voltage`: value 9, unit \"V\"\n  - `power_supply_type`: \"DC (external adapter); optional 6 V internal battery\"? That mixes. Better:\n  - `adapter_input_voltage`: {min:100, max:240}, \"V\"\n  - `adapter_input_frequency`: {min:50, max:60}, \"Hz\"\n  - `battery_voltage`: 6, \"V\"\n  \n  And power_supply_type: \"DC external adapter, optional internal battery\" as string. Hmm. I'll include power_supply_type: {value: \"DC\"} and the battery covers the